#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124277 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "033e459e-5148-5e78-ab5c-7e455b06cd64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3695" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{xz}{y}\\,j+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124278 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "f44b6ea6-694c-5f54-94ee-4c4a699bf4eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3696" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1346c66e-add9-58bb-914d-bde230ffb97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281874} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbc6691-be3c-5174-bc33-891dad11a619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282476} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6cbe6-66c9-5cdf-85f9-16bcc6c8db5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282765} {:answer/id #uuid "c8135d32-63d4-5f0e-ba7b-566e64774dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283438} {:answer/id #uuid "d748975e-258c-5fd7-a8c3-a751942b65a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6cbe6-66c9-5cdf-85f9-16bcc6c8db5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282765} :answer-field/id #uuid "771db56a-a84d-5c67-ab37-e76a138f7e66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78b6efe3-1fb5-5655-97e2-03def53db36e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167453" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\,j+t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+(2xy+{z}^{3})\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3},y=1,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}\\cdot 0+3{t}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439aee45-933e-5525-9345-5b17029a63c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282266} {:answer/id #uuid "582ea5c1-1e57-5176-9167-972093895fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282440} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb3e4fe-ec5b-5339-9652-4a5cb0a96a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282782} {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282831} {:answer/id #uuid "e9802ab6-19b7-5148-b0e2-623781519ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282831} :answer-field/id #uuid "353e1da0-d350-56e5-9994-3f64a6bca475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2df41f1-db14-5e76-bbfb-6cc0c8cd31a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167497" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282046} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1dd875-1ab5-5d5c-8cee-d4a760e0fad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282077} {:answer/id #uuid "36e21432-61e2-5f75-bf7b-6c17d3bac35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282176} {:answer/id #uuid "878e3c0b-0fa7-530a-8b50-3b0123c90209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282857} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ea2448-8707-56af-883d-3fdfe6fbad5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282046} :answer-field/id #uuid "99251eef-a601-5418-8667-74393704a93e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15c14f08-def7-5eb6-8ced-3b42bca65403" :question/math-academy-id "q-167498" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "945b9c04-6a85-5d2f-aac7-20fb300b68cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282964} {:answer/id #uuid "a0759ec4-44c2-5df4-8049-d2141efec4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283086} {:answer/id #uuid "cefe4dd8-87f4-5862-998f-f2f8be85aafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283496} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f7e03-4f52-5b2c-83bb-c9656091962b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283757} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bb78c7-3837-59e6-b577-9cf297b1d8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f7e03-4f52-5b2c-83bb-c9656091962b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283757} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53902b6-01db-5851-b51a-31934c273c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b00d973d-5785-5a66-98c1-0a412869e903" :question/math-academy-id "q-167499" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05130439-f8ad-50ab-88fd-4aef2c512276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321194} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab62fb4-96f6-544e-a43f-7c54848d7b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321475} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb77509-e2b1-57fe-aa81-c820e1759f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321484} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29a8cb-f3e2-55a6-b61e-7dcb1a986936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322877} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8e8763-9fc2-54d7-beb9-a7f164362324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd29a8cb-f3e2-55a6-b61e-7dcb1a986936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322877} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f23e9e-f039-5d42-b46f-a3ba271cfc87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceb16fee-64a8-51ee-a3b7-7009e970259a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36215" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(t)}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,i+\\frac{3}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{4{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}+\\frac{3}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a27301a5-c679-526f-8d17-1cbce40107f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{t + 1} + te^{t} + e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122355} {:answer/id #uuid "dc847600-a9fe-5d52-a83c-b7c1a9114503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{t + 1} + (1 + 2t)e^{t} + e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122356} {:answer/id #uuid "3de1efcc-6288-573e-8e67-2b7d12a0c3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{t + 1} + (1 + t)e^{t} + e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122357} {:answer/id #uuid "527630fa-6722-5392-9b51-faae42cd275f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{t} + 2e + t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122358} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef2fec2-232b-582f-aa15-ab3dc3313b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{t + 1} + (t + 1)e^{t} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122359}] :answer-field/id #uuid "00f9b55f-9f7a-5171-8a0f-7462d539b472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122354}] :question/id #uuid "f10e177e-2926-5cde-8e9a-4b0a99b2ff4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36238" :question/problem "If $f(x, y, z) = xy + yz + xz$ and $r(t) = ti + e^{t}j + ek$, compute $\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde96964-49e5-5f8c-9a85-bf83f3f624a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122361} {:answer/id #uuid "c18873f1-6416-5e3f-84a8-30f0d1e34a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122362} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8b5412-a282-5231-a52a-c430c381a304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122363} {:answer/id #uuid "55bd4f06-001b-5b40-9449-067e29d3fd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122364} {:answer/id #uuid "5c13ba27-5c90-535c-9ea9-9232444e86bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122365}] :answer-field/id #uuid "30ce640c-974a-53b3-8a92-6e2b4d383ffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122360}] :question/id #uuid "e234e1ce-e3a5-5460-9b85-a8876cb6e36c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36247" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x, y, z) = x^{2} + 3xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t) = ⟨t^{2}, t + 1,-t⟩$ at the point $t = 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1ec52e-ccdc-5b0f-b240-92720661c62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+9{t}^{3}+6{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321508} {:answer/id #uuid "5c35907b-6462-5098-94af-12ec14663f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-3t-3t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321877} {:answer/id #uuid "82fd3b74-e5d5-5eb9-84d2-cb77ba0c33b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-3{t}^{2}+36{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322179} {:answer/id #uuid "ac96cf45-01f8-5f94-b48d-b88b9ae7af6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{2}-12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322499} {:answer/id #uuid "d89116e6-d4ee-5b61-abf5-08714b411c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d89116e6-d4ee-5b61-abf5-08714b411c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842} :answer-field/id #uuid "d379d752-f7ce-576e-9786-94b4166ca354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1916a81-682a-57cf-b7a5-c43c18c456ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-37099" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2x-x{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2-{y}^{2})\\,i-2xy\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=3{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c36ce21-00fb-5fff-a854-09a22fe9b12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321487} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8cc371-8d3d-50ee-84ac-7aa30e752029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321639} {:answer/id #uuid "51cf7225-599b-5037-bcde-98e28ee76a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321791} {:answer/id #uuid "842b9eff-5030-5ffd-9f05-2ac210a4ff66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322191} {:answer/id #uuid "f84babd5-90be-52b2-8398-56d622fd27c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84babd5-90be-52b2-8398-56d622fd27c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323099} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9f0d4f-747d-5dbd-9a61-61b8b32ac61a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48ca6f9a-7d23-5701-9123-39d54477cf36" :question/math-academy-id "q-37109" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=0$ given that $∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}+1)\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}[(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =2{e}^{2(0)}((2(0)+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))+2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ecb641-5c9e-59d2-9af0-e8b4b2b12cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358237} {:answer/id #uuid "3913aa2e-745b-50de-97ac-427a88ec41d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358256} {:answer/id #uuid "58cc3bc9-ecef-54de-9839-9c06a26426b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358521} {:answer/id #uuid "996b9c09-c035-53fe-ba0a-1216f4518389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359098} {:answer/id #uuid "dd14b83a-9e9f-5129-b7b0-19250eeeb3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996b9c09-c035-53fe-ba0a-1216f4518389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359098} :answer-field/id #uuid "31bbcbbe-389e-5798-9aef-8032762b891a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53f0d6f9-0bd0-5fbc-870f-a5ea8fa6cdba" :question/math-academy-id "q-94887" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t},3t-1⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t,y=\\sqrt{t}$ and $z=3t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\sqrt{t}(3t-1),2t(3t-1),2t\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩ \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 2+(6{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+2t\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}+6t\\sqrt{t} \\\\  & =12t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =12(1)\\sqrt{1}-2\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{3(1)^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e78089-dc3f-5261-a0b9-838d20687d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357803} {:answer/id #uuid "50e71c08-98f9-50a2-a32b-d087d082f280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358447} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9f48cc-cae9-5cde-af64-d389fb6174af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358832} {:answer/id #uuid "b8de77d8-406c-5b9b-a817-38720fcff979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359354} {:answer/id #uuid "eec515f8-b1e7-52bb-b00a-3fe712040562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8de77d8-406c-5b9b-a817-38720fcff979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f2da6b-5bbf-5958-acfc-6db810c9ab3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "102aa53b-1989-56be-b06a-9a6f6770770c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94888" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,4,\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},3{y}^{2},2xz⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=4$ and $z=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3})^{2},3(4)^{2},2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-3)\\cdot 1+48\\cdot 0+2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3+0+2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =3(2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =12-3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0661fd29-a292-5ab4-b31a-2396be427221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357808} {:answer/id #uuid "2c19e560-7929-538c-bcc2-9884cff52e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} {:answer/id #uuid "bb354d15-c911-57ac-961b-2bf623e8c47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359378} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd453d2-7215-5f3b-8c26-6cee0b42f4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359383} {:answer/id #uuid "caaabb05-4b0e-56ee-9471-80ef684ce597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c19e560-7929-538c-bcc2-9884cff52e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c5c1098-6416-500c-946c-2648c90f4466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eac0cd5-9d30-54ff-89b8-2cfd1b9003d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95052" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)=\\pi t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\pi t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j)\\cdot (\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\pi {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e56dc23-76eb-5db9-9fce-e4d36ef6a300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3({t}^{2}+1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358426} {:answer/id #uuid "50d39335-f934-5320-9bd6-5f69635a09d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}{e}^{t}+2}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358445} {:answer/id #uuid "7dab4a7a-ef04-5286-8145-c994f7fd0f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} {:answer/id #uuid "de80fcbe-829a-5dae-a81d-fb3cf700a555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359706} {:answer/id #uuid "eb612ba8-e57f-520d-9b63-e9ab14d4e478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t{e}^{t}-1)}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dab4a7a-ef04-5286-8145-c994f7fd0f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed81ec3-d906-5a86-bc7c-31b1ba6834fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99c33c9d-4cdd-5e75-a91b-a13201898eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-95054" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={t}^{-2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{2}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{-2}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{-2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{e}^{t}}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{-2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-2({t}^{2})({t}^{-3})+2{e}^{-t}({e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a652f4fa-5e51-550b-ae0f-acf975b5ad30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t + 1}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124095} {:answer/id #uuid "c951b256-32ff-5f68-b860-7cb5bb3678fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 - t\\tan t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124096} {:answer/id #uuid "d94d7c62-b302-5dfb-b7aa-0f1dc5f7e8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^{2}\\tan t + 2}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124097} {:answer/id #uuid "26161a3b-9d9f-5127-866f-e9b489af75b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 - t\\sec t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124098} {:answer/id #uuid "2e51b451-2a42-5fa4-9919-4c8fb375df52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^{2}\\sec t + 1}{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124099}] :answer-field/id #uuid "00f4d712-e22f-5cf5-b66e-67d08872e484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124094}] :question/id #uuid "72c45146-95bd-53af-b2f0-49b5ef23b12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95056" :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = \\ln xy$ and $r(t) = \\cos ti + t^{2}j$, compute $\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043ac664-b524-524e-aa72-bf1d30123527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357793} {:answer/id #uuid "af3ddaa4-8953-5d46-9032-e2f95ff616b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t{cos}^{3}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359275} {:answer/id #uuid "c43dfc74-6743-5922-a14e-6c2caaeb73de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359473} {:answer/id #uuid "cc97c0a8-0127-5bdc-a492-17ba4f68be95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359546} {:answer/id #uuid "da9c33c8-b2a5-5f98-b17d-5810e16dbeda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043ac664-b524-524e-aa72-bf1d30123527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357793} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9feea77-0770-57d9-9bdc-bf443c95c674" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a60dcabe-5a9f-584e-9268-2f3213ceda5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95070" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,k \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fb2e01-985f-513d-813b-cfde3f1b377e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358661} {:answer/id #uuid "788c776c-5597-51d2-9394-be9dfb7f75ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358792} {:answer/id #uuid "901c96ff-6cee-55e9-8211-cd0101addfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359007} {:answer/id #uuid "a22be918-009f-5e59-9d70-9b6808c8792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359167} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc10872-0c59-572a-92f2-5c00d7f80870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbc10872-0c59-572a-92f2-5c00d7f80870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359542} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef695bf-af69-5590-8e72-7ce89ea59101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee940918-1cef-5c23-a56c-5afe1494fd57" :question/math-academy-id "q-95146" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,k \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y={e}^{t},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
